Tycoon 2FA operators did not move away from the credential-relay kit: eSentire still names the OfficeHome AppId as the primary marker of the relay variant and treats it and the Broker AppId as one detection set, and BleepingComputer reports that the kit now supports device-code phishing ([eSentire TRU, 2026-05-12](https://www.esentire.com/blog/tycoon-2fa-operators-adopt-oauth-device-code-phishing); [BleepingComputer, 2026-05-17](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/tycoon2fa-hijacks-microsoft-365-accounts-via-device-code-phishing/)). The Alibaba (US) hosting seen since about 2026-04-10 is an addition to the hosting providers documented before, not a replacement ([eSentire TRU, 2026-05-12](https://www.esentire.com/blog/tycoon-2fa-operators-adopt-oauth-device-code-phishing)). The lure page shows a user code obtained by the operator's backend and tells the victim to paste it at microsoft.com/devicelogin; it does not carry the quoted instruction text given earlier ([BleepingComputer, 2026-05-17](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/tycoon2fa-hijacks-microsoft-365-accounts-via-device-code-phishing/)). The detection signals eSentire publishes are successful deviceCode sign-ins from sources with no prior device-code activity, tokens issued to the Microsoft Authentication Broker AppId, node or undici user agents in non-interactive sign-ins, and ResultType 50173 after revocation ([eSentire TRU, 2026-05-12](https://www.esentire.com/blog/tycoon-2fa-operators-adopt-oauth-device-code-phishing)). eSentire says the phish does not bypass MFA but changes what MFA authorizes, and recommends blocking the device-code flow with Conditional Access as the control that removes the attack class ([eSentire TRU, 2026-05-12](https://www.esentire.com/blog/tycoon-2fa-operators-adopt-oauth-device-code-phishing)), in line with Microsoft's advice to get as close as possible to a unilateral block ([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/policy-block-authentication-flows)); no source says that FIDO2 sign-in defeats device-code phishing.
